      "narrative": "This is a literature screening alert summarising a published journal article. The alert provides bibliographic information (title, authors, journal, DOI) but does not describe any individual patient case or adverse reaction. The full text of the article is referenced as attached but is not included in the source material provided for extraction. No adverse events, patient identifiers, or clinical details are present in this alert itself.",
      "fields": [
        {
          "field_group": "NARRATIVE",
          "field_path": "narrative",
          "value": "Three patients developed adverse reactions during treatment with velmoradine or related agents. Case 1: A 58-year-old female with hypertension and type 2 diabetes was prescribed Velmoradine 20 mg once daily starting 3 March 2026. Nine days later she developed a widespread itchy maculopapular rash across the trunk and both forearms. The drug was withdrawn on 13 March and the rash began to settle within 48 hours without corticosteroid treatment. Case 2: A 29-year-old pregnant woman at 22 weeks gestation had been taking Domitrelle 2 mg at night for generalised anxiety disorder since early 2026. In July she developed an erythematous eruption on sun-exposed skin after only ten minutes outdoors. The pregnancy remained ongoing with no fetal abnormality detected on ultrasound. Case 3: A woman born in 1966 with a history of migraine with aura was prescribed Fenaquil 500 mg as required for acute migraine starting last March. About two weeks later she developed light-headedness on standing, worse in the mornings. The symptom improved but had not fully resolved at the time of reporting.",
          "typed_value": null,
